      {
        "id": "p22-f3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain what the ambiguous case of the sine rule is.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Two different triangles satisfy the same given dimensions.",
          "worked": "Two different triangles can have the same two side lengths and non-included angle. This happens when both an angle $\\theta$ and its supplement $180^\\circ-\\theta$ produce valid triangles.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-f3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "When can it occur?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "When two side lengths and a non-included angle are given and the sine rule is used to find an unknown angle.",
          "worked": "With two sides and a non-included angle, the sine rule can give two candidate angles because $\\sin\\theta=\\sin(180^\\circ-\\theta)$. The ambiguous case occurs if both candidates satisfy the triangle angle-sum requirement.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-f3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p22-f3-c-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "When using the sine rule to solve for an a",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "ngle",
                  "width": 15,
                  "joinPrevious": true
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", you cannot simply accept the first solution ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\theta",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " from your calculator. You must also check if the angle ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$180^\\circ-\\theta$",
                  "width": 17
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " also works.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "angle; $180^\\circ-\\theta$",
          "worked": "The two candidates have the same sine:\n$$\\sin(180^\\circ-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta.$$\nCheck whether the supplementary angle also gives a valid triangle. If $\\theta=90^\\circ$, its supplement is the same angle.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0,
        "responseSpace": "scaffold"
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-f3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p22-f3-d-text",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "To check if the other angle ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$180^\\circ-\\theta$",
                  "width": 17
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " also works, add it to the other known angle in the triangle, and see if the sum of the angles is greater than or equal to ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "cloze",
                  "answer": "$180^\\circ$",
                  "width": 15
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". If it is, then accept/reject (circle one) that second answer.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$180^\\circ-\\theta$; $180^\\circ$; reject",
          "worked": "Add $180^\\circ-\\theta$ to the other known angle. Reject the second candidate if their sum is at least $180^\\circ$, because the third angle would be zero or negative. A valid triangle requires their sum to be less than $180^\\circ$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
